import{J as e,n as t,ot as n,q as r}from"./chunks/framework.CF9rEaS5.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Share one UDP port between RakNet and NetherNet in native code","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Share one UDP port between RakNet and NetherNet in native code"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":""}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://jsprismarine.dev/adr/0008-shared-udp-port-in-native-code"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://jsprismarine.dev/adr/0008-shared-udp-port-in-native-code"}]],"editSource":{"kind":"file","url":"https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/edit/master/docs/adr/0008-shared-udp-port-in-native-code.md"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0008-shared-udp-port-in-native-code.md","filePath":"adr/0008-shared-udp-port-in-native-code.md","lastUpdated":1794449216000}`),a={name:`adr/0008-shared-udp-port-in-native-code.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<div><h1 id="share-one-udp-port-between-raknet-and-nethernet-in-native-code" tabindex="-1">Share one UDP port between RakNet and NetherNet in native code <a class="header-anchor" href="#share-one-udp-port-between-raknet-and-nethernet-in-native-code" aria-label="Permalink to “Share one UDP port between RakNet and NetherNet in native code”">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted.</p><p>Bedrock Dedicated Server 1.26.70 uses <code>server-port</code> for NetherNet&#39;s UDP when <code>server-udp-ports</code> is unset. It can, because it runs NetherNet alone. We run RakNet and NetherNet together by default, and RakNet also wants that UDP port. Our native networking library owns the shared port through libjuice, libdatachannel&#39;s ICE library, and sorts each datagram by its structure before libjuice looks it up. RakNet&#39;s datagrams go to RakNet through a shared-memory ring, and WebRTC&#39;s stay in native code. WebRTC traffic never enters JavaScript, and RakNet&#39;s traffic enters it in batches.</p><p>The research behind it is in <a href="/pr-preview/pr-3055/research/nethernet-raknet-shared-port.html">the research note</a>, which built and ran a JavaScript relay first. That relay is the alternative below.</p><h2 id="why-native-code-owns-the-port" tabindex="-1">Why native code owns the port <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-native-code-owns-the-port" aria-label="Permalink to “Why native code owns the port”">​</a></h2><p>libdatachannel&#39;s point is that ICE, DTLS and SCTP run in native code, off the event loop. A relay in JavaScript puts each WebRTC datagram through the event loop twice, once received from the public socket and once sent to libjuice over loopback, for three system calls where libjuice alone makes one. RakNet&#39;s datagrams are handled in JavaScript in any case, so moving the classification to the native side costs RakNet nothing it does not already pay, and frees WebRTC of the event loop.</p><h2 id="why-we-sort-by-structure-first" tabindex="-1">Why we sort by structure first <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-we-sort-by-structure-first" aria-label="Permalink to “Why we sort by structure first”">​</a></h2><p>libjuice already sorts datagrams on its mux socket: by source address for a known peer, then by the username fragment in a STUN Binding request. Whatever matches no peer is dropped (<code>conn_mux_process</code> in <code>src/conn_mux.c</code>). Routing RakNet through that lookup works, and it costs more than it looks:</p><ul><li>Every RakNet datagram is a miss. The address table is open addressing with linear probing (<code>find_map_entry</code>), and a miss scans to the next empty slot.</li><li>A peer that leaves turns its slots into tombstones (<code>MAP_ENTRY_TYPE_DELETED</code> in <code>remove_map_entries</code>), and only a resize clears them. On a server where players come and go, a miss scans past more tombstones over time.</li><li>The lookup runs under the registry&#39;s mutex, which libjuice&#39;s sends for every peer also take.</li></ul><p>The protocols on the port tell themselves apart by their bytes (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7983" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RFC 7983</a> for STUN and DTLS). So our C layer classifies each datagram first, on libjuice&#39;s thread, and only the ones that can be WebRTC reach the lookup:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Checked</th><th>Bytes</th><th>Class</th><th>Goes to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>First byte <code>0x80</code> to <code>0xFF</code></td><td>Connected RakNet (frame sets, ACK, NACK), and query at <code>0xFE 0xFD</code></td><td>RakNet</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>First byte <code>0x01</code> or <code>0x02</code>, with RakNet&#39;s offline magic at offset 9</td><td>Unconnected ping</td><td>RakNet</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>First byte <code>0x05</code> or <code>0x07</code>, with the offline magic at offset 1</td><td>Open connection request</td><td>RakNet</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>First byte 0 to 3, with STUN&#39;s cookie at bytes 4 to 7 and a length that is a multiple of 4</td><td>STUN</td><td>libjuice</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>First byte 20 to 63</td><td>DTLS</td><td>libjuice</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Anything else</td><td>Unsure</td><td>libjuice, then RakNet if libjuice claims it for no peer</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The rules run in that order, and the first that matches decides. A STUN success response also starts <code>0x01</code>, and a RakNet ping can carry STUN&#39;s cookie at bytes 4 to 7 by chance, so the RakNet magic is checked before the cookie. A STUN message that carried the 16-byte magic at offset 9 would go to RakNet, which drops it.</p><p>Almost all of RakNet&#39;s traffic is connected datagrams, which the first byte settles. Only an unsure datagram pays for the lookup, and RakNet&#39;s protocol code ignores what is not RakNet.</p><h2 id="what-we-build" tabindex="-1">What we build <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-we-build" aria-label="Permalink to “What we build”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Three additions to libjuice.</strong> A filter callback that sees each datagram before the lookup and says whether libjuice keeps it, a callback for each datagram the mux claims for no peer, and a function that sends a datagram from the mux socket to an address. The classifier itself is in our C layer, so the patch stays generic. The mux socket is one dual-stack socket (<code>IPV6_V6ONLY</code> off in <code>src/udp.c</code>), so it serves RakNet&#39;s IPv4 and IPv6 clients on the shared number too. We carry the change as a patch on the libjuice we build, and offer it upstream.</p><p><strong>Rings between the native side and RakNet.</strong> The classifier copies each RakNet datagram, with its address, into an inbound ring in shared memory, and wakes the consumer once per batch rather than once per datagram. RakNet writes outgoing datagrams into an outbound ring, which the native side sends from the mux socket. With the RakNet worker, the worker is the consumer and the producer, so the main thread sees none of it. <code>SharedByteRing</code> in <code>packages/raknet</code> already carries datagrams between the worker and the main thread in this shape.</p><p><strong>A datagram source in <code>packages/raknet</code>.</strong> <code>ServerSocket</code> reads from and writes to a source: a Node.js UDP socket, as today, or the native rings. RakNet&#39;s protocol code does not change. Query, which already answers on RakNet&#39;s socket, comes with it.</p><p><strong>Only where the ports collide.</strong> The native side owns a NetherNet port that equals <code>port</code> or <code>server-portv6</code> while RakNet is on: a single port, or the one port of a range that equals either. RakNet keeps its own Node.js sockets for any port it does not share. With RakNet off, nothing changes for libjuice.</p><h2 id="what-it-costs" tabindex="-1">What it costs <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-costs" aria-label="Permalink to “What it costs”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Our own build of libjuice and libdatachannel, for every platform we ship, since a prebuilt node-datachannel cannot take the patch. <a href="/pr-preview/pr-3055/adr/0006-libdatachannel-for-nethernet-server.html">ADR 0006</a> makes that build the only driver, so this adds the patch and the rings, not the build.</li><li>A thread boundary in RakNet&#39;s path when the port is shared: one copy into the ring per datagram, and one wake per batch. A long tick no longer delays WebRTC, and it still delays RakNet as it does today.</li><li>Two kinds of datagram source in RakNet, both under its tests.</li><li>An unsure datagram from an address libjuice has mapped goes to that peer. Connected RakNet never reaches the lookup, so this only touches a client that sends both transports from one socket.</li><li>The patch stays ours until libjuice takes it.</li></ul><h2 id="alternatives-not-taken" tabindex="-1">Alternatives not taken <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-not-taken" aria-label="Permalink to “Alternatives not taken”">​</a></h2><p><strong>A JavaScript relay.</strong> RakNet&#39;s socket sorts each datagram by its first bytes and relays STUN and DTLS over loopback to libjuice on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, with the answer&#39;s candidates rewritten. It needs no native change, and the research note ran it: werift clients moved data through one port while it answered RakNet pings. It puts every WebRTC datagram through the event loop twice, which undoes what libdatachannel is for.</p><p><strong>Keep the two port numbers apart by default.</strong> It needs none of this. It gives up BDS&#39;s default, and every server that offers both transports needs two UDP ports open. It is the fallback if a Bedrock client refuses the shared port.</p><p><strong><code>SO_REUSEPORT</code> with both sockets on the port.</strong> The kernel splits datagrams by a hash of the sender, not by content. In a run, 200 datagrams split 110 and 90. Steering by content needs BPF, which is Linux only.</p><p><strong>Route everything through libjuice&#39;s lookup</strong>, with only the drop turned into a callback. It is a smaller patch, and it makes every RakNet datagram pay for a miss that grows with player churn.</p><p><strong>A libjuice connection mode with no socket of its own</strong>, fed by us. libjuice&#39;s modes are a table of functions (<code>conn.h</code>), so it is possible. It moves the socket into our code and every receive into JavaScript or a second native component, for no gain over the callback.</p><h2 id="room-for-a-native-raknet" tabindex="-1">Room for a native RakNet <a class="header-anchor" href="#room-for-a-native-raknet" aria-label="Permalink to “Room for a native RakNet”">​</a></h2><p>RakNet in native code is not part of this decision, and the design leaves room for it. The classifier hands RakNet&#39;s datagrams to a destination in the C layer, which is the rings today. A native RakNet would be another destination in the same library, and would hand the server sessions and frames instead of datagrams. On the JavaScript side, <code>ServerSocket</code>&#39;s datagram source is the seam today, and a native RakNet would replace the whole RakNet transport behind the server&#39;s transport interface, as NetherNet sits behind it.</p></div>`,1)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};